MVM.registerModule({
  "id": "m19",
  "summary": "Explain how hydraulic brakes, ABS and wheel bearings work, service brakes safely and correctly, and diagnose brake faults without ever returning a doubtful vehicle to the road.",
  "outcomes": ["Explain the hydraulic brake system, the booster and the properties of brake fluid.","Describe disc and drum brakes, the parking brake and the wear limits.","Replace pads and discs, bleed the system and test the brakes after service.","Explain how ABS and stability control work and what their warning lights mean.","Diagnose spongy and hard pedals, pulling, judder, noise and drag.","Describe wheel bearing types, check play and noise, and replace a hub bearing with the correct torque."],
  "cheatsheet": ["Hydraulic brakes work because liquid cannot be compressed. Pressure is the same everywhere in the fluid.","Brake fluid absorbs water from the air. Water lowers the boiling point and causes corrosion. Change it on schedule.","Use only the DOT grade on the reservoir cap or in the manual. Never mix silicone DOT 5 with glycol fluids.","Replace pads and discs in axle sets. Never change only one side.","Never grease the friction surfaces. Lubricate slides and pins only with brake grease.","A spongy pedal usually means air in the system. A hard pedal suggests a booster or vacuum fault.","ABS keeps the wheels turning in hard braking so you can still steer. It does not shorten every stop.","Do not blow brake dust with compressed air. Use a brake cleaner or a damp cloth and wear a mask.","After any brake work, pump the pedal, test at low speed and tell the customer to brake gently for the bedding-in distance."],
  "lessons": [
    {"id": "m19-l1", "title": "Hydraulic brake system", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Brakes are the most important safety system on the vehicle. A mechanic who does brake work carries a heavy responsibility, so you must understand the system and follow the manual. This lesson starts with how the hydraulic system works."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"hydraulic-brake-circuit"},
      {"t":"h","text":"The hydraulic principle"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Liquids cannot be compressed. When the driver pushes on the pedal, a piston in the **master cylinder** pushes fluid through the pipes. The pressure in the fluid is the same everywhere in the closed system. The force that a piston gives depends on its area:"},
      {"t":"formula","text":"Force = Pressure × Area","caption":"A small piston at the master cylinder makes pressure. A bigger piston at the caliper or wheel cylinder turns the same pressure into a bigger force."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Main parts"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Brake pedal and linkage:** a lever that multiplies the force of the driver's foot.","**Brake booster:** a vacuum or electric assistance that adds force so that the pedal is light. A vacuum booster uses the difference between engine vacuum and atmospheric pressure. Diesel engines and electric vehicles use a vacuum pump or an electric pump.","**Master cylinder:** a tandem cylinder with two circuits, so if one fails the other still works. A reservoir on top supplies the fluid.","**Lines and hoses:** steel pipes carry the fluid along the body. Flexible rubber hoses connect to the moving wheels.","**Calipers and wheel cylinders:** convert fluid pressure into force at the brake.","**Proportioning valve:** reduces the pressure to the rear brakes, because weight moves forward in braking. Many modern cars do this through ABS."]},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"brake-booster"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Brake fluid"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Brake fluid is hygroscopic, which means it absorbs water from the air. Water lowers the boiling point. In hard braking, the heat can boil the wet fluid and make vapour bubbles that can be compressed, and the pedal goes to the floor. The common grades are **DOT 3, DOT 4 and DOT 5.1**, which are glycol based and can be mixed in some cases, and **DOT 5**, which is silicone based and must not be mixed with the others. Use only the grade shown on the cap or in the manual. Brake fluid also damages paint, so wipe spills at once."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Brake pipes and hoses"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Part","Material","Points to check"],"rows":[["Rigid brake pipe","Steel coated with zinc, or copper-nickel alloy","Rust, dents, kinks, wear where it rubs a clip, and leaking joints"],["Flexible hose","Reinforced rubber","Cracks, bulges, chafing, twists, and a hose that swells under pedal pressure"],["Unions and flares","The pipe ends are flared (a double or a bubble flare) to seal in the fitting","Use the right flare type. A wrong one leaks."],["Junctions and valves","Brass or steel blocks","Leaks and corrosion"]]},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Replace a corroded pipe with a new one of the same material and diameter. **Never** use plumbing copper, plastic or a poor flare.","Do not join a damaged pipe with a compression fitting. Fit a new section with correct flares.","Fit hoses without twisting them, and make sure they do not touch the tyre or the arm on full lock and full bump.","Tighten unions to the specified torque. Over-tightening splits the flare, and under-tightening leaks."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Fluid cannot be compressed but air can. Any air in the system gives a soft pedal and longer stopping distances."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Check the level and the colour of the fluid on every service. Dark fluid or a level that falls shows age or a leak."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m19-l2", "title": "Braking physics: stopping distance, energy and weight transfer", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Brakes turn the energy of a moving vehicle into heat. How far a vehicle takes to stop depends on its speed, the grip of the tyres and the driver's reaction. This lesson gives the numbers behind these ideas, so that a technician can explain to a customer why worn tyres or bad brakes are dangerous, and can see what a brake system has to do."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Stopping distance"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **stopping distance** is the sum of the **thinking distance**, how far the vehicle travels while the driver reacts, and the **braking distance**, how far it travels while it slows down."},
      {"t":"formula","text":"Braking distance = v² ÷ (2 × μ × g)","caption":"v is the speed in metres per second, μ is the friction between tyre and road (about 0.8 on dry asphalt, 0.5 on wet, 0.1 on ice), and g is 9.81 m/s²"},
      {"t":"example","title":"Stopping from 100 km/h","steps":["100 km/h is 27.8 m/s. A reaction time of 1.0 second gives a thinking distance of 27.8 m.","On dry asphalt, μ = 0.8. Braking distance = 27.8² ÷ (2 × 0.8 × 9.81) = 772.8 ÷ 15.7 = 49.2 m. The total is 27.8 + 49.2 = 77.0 m.","On a wet road, μ = 0.5. Braking distance = 772.8 ÷ 9.81 = 78.8 m. The total is 27.8 + 78.8 = 106.6 m. That is about 30 m, or seven car lengths, further than the dry stop.","Worn tyres lower μ on a wet road to perhaps 0.35, giving a braking distance of 772.8 ÷ 6.87 = 112.5 m. The vehicle may not stop in time."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Speed matters most"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Speed","Dry braking distance (μ = 0.8)","Thinking distance (1 s)","Total"],"rows":[["30 km/h","About 4.4 m","About 8.3 m","About 13 m"],["50 km/h","About 12.3 m","About 13.9 m","About 26 m"],["80 km/h","About 31.4 m","About 22.2 m","About 54 m"],["100 km/h","About 49.2 m","About 27.8 m","About 77 m"],["120 km/h","About 70.8 m","About 33.3 m","About 104 m"]],"caption":"Braking distance rises with the square of speed. Double the speed and the distance is four times as long."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The energy to be removed"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The kinetic energy of a vehicle is ½ × mass × speed². The brakes must turn this into heat."},
      {"t":"example","title":"How much heat is made?","steps":["A car of 1,400 kg at 27.8 m/s (100 km/h) has an energy of 0.5 × 1,400 × 772.8 = 541,000 J, or 541 kJ.","A stop with a deceleration of 0.8 g (7.85 m/s²) takes 27.8 ÷ 7.85 = 3.5 seconds.","The average braking power is 541 ÷ 3.5 = 153 kW, as much as the engine of a powerful car, but for a short time.","If 70% goes to the front brakes, 107 kW goes into two front discs, about 54 kW each. The discs can glow red on long hills. If a disc is thin or the pads poor, the heat cannot be absorbed and the brakes **fade**."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Weight transfer when braking"},
      {"t":"p","text":"When you brake, the weight of the car shifts forward. The front tyres carry more load, and the rear tyres less. The front brakes must therefore do more of the work, which is why front brakes are larger and why the rear brakes must not lock first."},
      {"t":"example","title":"How much weight shifts to the front?","steps":["A car of 1,400 kg brakes at 0.8 g. The centre of gravity is 0.55 m high and the wheelbase is 2.6 m.","Weight transfer = mass × deceleration × height ÷ wheelbase = 1,400 × 7.85 × 0.55 ÷ 2.6 = 2,325 N, or 237 kg.","If the car carries 55% of its weight on the front axle at rest, it carries about 770 kg + 237 kg = 1,007 kg on the front during the stop. The rear carries 630 − 237 = 393 kg.","Ideal braking would put about 72% of the braking force on the front. A **proportioning valve**, or ABS electronic brake force distribution, makes sure that the rear brakes get a smaller share, so that they do not lock first."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Brake fade and recovery"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Pad fade:** the friction material gets so hot that its friction falls. The pedal goes hard, and the stopping distance increases.","**Fluid fade:** the brake fluid boils from heat and makes vapour bubbles. The pedal goes soft and may go to the floor. Old, wet fluid boils at a lower temperature.","**Recovery:** after cooling, pads and fluid may recover, but pads may be glazed or damaged and fluid may be ruined.","Use a lower gear on long hills. Engine braking saves the brakes."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Speed, grip and heat decide how well brakes work. A bad tyre, old fluid or a worn pad all make the stopping distance longer, and the effect is greatest at high speed and in the wet."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Showing a customer the stopping distance table is a good way to explain why new tyres or fluid matter."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m19-l3", "title": "Disc and drum brakes", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Brakes change the energy of movement into heat by friction. The two main types are disc and drum. This lesson explains how each works and when to replace the parts."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"disc-and-drum-brakes"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Disc brakes"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **disc** (rotor) turns with the wheel. A **caliper** straddles it and holds two **pads**. When the driver brakes, the hydraulic piston in the caliper pushes the pads against the disc. Discs are open to the air, so they cool well, and they are used on the front of nearly all cars and on the rear of many."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Fixed caliper:** pistons on both sides of the disc.","**Floating (sliding) caliper:** one side has the piston and the caliper slides on pins to clamp the other pad. The slide pins must be clean and lubricated, or the pad wears unevenly.","**Ventilated discs:** have vanes between two faces for better cooling."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Drum brakes"},
      {"t":"p","text":"In a **drum** brake, two curved **shoes** inside a drum are pushed outwards by a **wheel cylinder** to rub on the inside of the drum. They are enclosed, cheap, and work well as parking brakes, so they are common on the rear of small cars and trucks. They have return springs and an **automatic adjuster** that keeps the shoe clearance small as the lining wears."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Parking brake"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The handbrake (parking brake) works the rear brakes through cables or by an electric motor. On discs, it may use a small drum inside the disc, or a mechanism in the caliper. A cable that is seized or badly adjusted gives a handbrake that does not hold or a rear brake that drags."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Wear limits and noise"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Replace pads when the friction material is at the minimum thickness in the manual, often about 3 mm. Some pads have a wear indicator that squeals.","Discs and drums have a minimum thickness or maximum diameter marked on them. Do not machine below it.","A grinding noise means the pad material is gone and metal is rubbing on the disc. Stop driving and repair."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Friction materials"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Type","Features","Notes"],"rows":[["Organic (non-asbestos)","Soft and quiet, with less dust","Wears faster, fades at high heat. Common on small cars."],["Semi-metallic","Fibres of steel and copper in a resin binder","Strong, good heat conduction, more noise and disc wear"],["Ceramic","Ceramic fibres, low dust and quiet","Stable and long-lasting. More expensive."],["Low-metallic","A mix with some metal","Good friction, can be noisy"]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Always fit pads of the type and quality the manufacturer recommends, or better. Pads of a different friction level can unbalance the front and rear of the vehicle. A cheap pad with a poor friction coefficient or a poor backing plate causes noise, fade and long stopping distances. Pads are fitted with **shims**, **clips** and sometimes a **wear sensor**. Replace these when the pads are replaced."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Common causes of brake noise"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Noise","Likely cause"],"rows":[["High squeal when braking lightly","Vibration of the pad on the disc. Dry slides, missing shims, glazed pads, or a wear indicator touching."],["Squeal when not braking","A pad touching the disc because a caliper is sticking or a clip is missing"],["Grinding","Worn-out friction material, metal on metal"],["Clunk when changing from forward to reverse","Pads loose in the bracket, worn or missing clips"],["Judder through the pedal","Disc thickness variation or run-out"],["Rattle from the rear drum","Broken return spring or loose shoe"]]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Old brake dust can contain harmful fibres. Do not blow it off with an air line and do not breathe it in. Use brake cleaner or a damp cloth and wear a dust mask."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Pads and discs wear in axle sets. Replace both sides together, or the car will pull to one side when braking."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m19-l4", "title": "Brake hydraulics and force calculations", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The hydraulic brake multiplies the force of the driver's foot many times. Working through the numbers shows how big the multiplication is, what limits it, and why a small leak or a worn part makes a big change in the pedal. It also shows why the brake pedal must never be pushed hard without a servo. This lesson follows the force through the system."},
      {"t":"h","text":"From foot to pad"},
      {"t":"example","title":"The path of a braking force","steps":["**Pedal:** the driver pushes with 150 N. The pedal has a lever ratio of 4:1, so the force on the pushrod is 600 N.","**Vacuum servo:** it assists by a factor of 4. The force at the master cylinder is 600 × 4 = 2,400 N (the servo supplies 1,800 N of it).","**Master cylinder:** its bore is 23.8 mm, with an area of 445 mm². Pressure = 2,400 ÷ 445 = 5.39 N/mm², or 53.9 bar.","**Caliper:** the piston is 54 mm in diameter, with an area of 2,290 mm². Clamping force = 5.39 × 2,290 = 12,340 N.","**Disc:** the pad friction coefficient is 0.40, and there are two friction faces. Friction force = 0.40 × 12,340 × 2 = 9,870 N. At an effective radius of 0.12 m, the torque on the disc is 1,184 N·m.","**Tyre:** the rolling radius is 0.316 m, so the braking force from this wheel is 1,184 ÷ 0.316 = 3,750 N."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"The two front wheels together give 7,500 N of braking force, which on a 1,400 kg car is 5.4 m/s² or 0.55 g from the front brakes alone. The rear brakes give the rest to reach 0.8 g. The pressure in the system is above 50 bar in a firm stop, and above 100 bar in an emergency stop. This is why all brake parts must be strong, and why pipes and hoses cannot be repaired by amateur methods."},
      {"t":"h","text":"What the servo does"},
      {"t":"p","text":"In this example, the driver supplies 600 N and the servo 1,800 N, which is three times as much. Without the servo, the driver would need 4 × the force, 600 N at the foot, which is about 60 kg of push. This is possible for a strong adult, but not in a long, repeated stop. If the vacuum is lost, the brakes still work but the pedal is hard, and the stopping distance is longer, because most drivers cannot push as hard as the servo does. This is why a hard pedal after the engine stops, or a leak in the vacuum hose, must be dealt with."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Pedal travel"},
      {"t":"example","title":"Why a soft pedal has long travel","steps":["The brake system must move a small volume of fluid to bring the pads to the disc. A caliper piston with an area of 2,290 mm² moving 0.2 mm, in four calipers, takes 2,290 × 0.2 × 4 = 1,832 mm³ of fluid, about 1.8 cm³.","The master cylinder has an area of 445 mm², so each piston moves 1,832 ÷ 445 = 4.1 mm. With the pedal ratio of 4, the pedal moves 16 mm.","If air bubbles in the fluid compress, they take up more of the movement. A bubble of 1 cm³ at 50 bar is squeezed to a fraction of its volume, and more pedal travel is needed to bring the pressure up. The pedal feels spongy and travels far."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Brake force distribution"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Device","How it works","Notes"],"rows":[["Fixed proportioning valve","Reduces the rear pressure above a set point","Cannot adjust to the load"],["Load-sensing valve","A linkage from the rear axle reduces the rear pressure for a light load and lets more through when loaded","Needs checking for rust and correct setting. Adjust only as the manual says."],["Electronic brake force distribution (EBD)","The ABS computer controls the rear pressure using the wheel speed sensors","No parts to adjust. A fault shows on the ABS light."]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"A vehicle with an adjustable load-sensing valve that is seized or wrongly set may lock the rear wheels in an emergency stop. A vehicle carrying a very heavy load needs the correct valve settings."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Pressure tests"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Holding test:** press the pedal firmly and hold it for 30 seconds. The pedal should not sink. A sinking pedal shows a leak at a seal or a pipe, or a leaking master cylinder.","**Servo test:** with the engine off, press the pedal several times to use up the vacuum. Hold the pedal down and start the engine. The pedal should sink a little, showing the servo working.","**Fluid pressure test:** a gauge is fitted at a bleed nipple, and the pressure measured as the pedal is pressed, in a workshop with the right equipment.","**Brake pedal travel:** the pedal should be firm after a short stroke. A pedal that goes more than half way, or to the floor, is unsafe."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"The pedal force is multiplied by the lever, the servo and the hydraulics. A fault in any one makes the pedal feel wrong, and it should never be ignored."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never drive a vehicle with a brake pedal that sinks, a brake fluid leak or a fault in the dual-circuit warning. Tow it, or repair it on the spot."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m19-l5", "title": "Brake service", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Brake service is the most common safety job in a workshop. Follow the steps in order, use clean hands and clean parts, and do not take short cuts."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"brake-wear"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Replacing pads and discs"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Chock the rear wheels, raise the vehicle on a lift or on stands and remove the wheel.","Inspect the caliper, hoses, pads and disc. Measure the pad thickness and the disc thickness and runout.","Remove the caliper bolts and hang the caliper from the spring or a hook. **Never let it hang on the brake hose.**","Push the piston back slowly with the correct tool. Watch the reservoir level, because the fluid rises.","Clean the caliper bracket and the slide pins. Lubricate the **pins** with the grease that is made for brakes. Do not grease the friction surfaces.","Fit new pads with their clips and shims. Replace the disc when it is under the limit, deeply grooved, cracked or warped.","Refit the caliper and tighten bolts to the torque in the manual, then the wheel nuts in a star pattern.","Press the brake pedal several times until it is firm before moving the vehicle."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Bleeding the brakes"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Bleeding removes air from the fluid. Start with the wheel that is **furthest** from the master cylinder, unless the manual says differently. Use new fluid of the right grade and never let the reservoir run empty."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Two-person method:** one presses the pedal and holds, the other opens and closes the bleed screw.","**One-person bleeder:** a pressure bleeder or a vacuum pump or a one-way valve.","Close the screw before the pedal is released. Continue until clear fluid with no bubbles comes out."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Bedding in and test"},
      {"t":"p","text":"New pads and discs need **bedding in** over the first few hundred kilometres with moderate braking. Test at low speed in a safe place. The pedal must be firm, the car must stop in a straight line, and there must be no leaks or noises."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Measuring a disc"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Measurement","Typical limit","What it shows"],"rows":[["Thickness","The minimum is stamped on the disc (for example MIN TH 22.0 mm)","A disc below the limit overheats and can crack"],["Thickness variation (DTV)","No more than about 0.01 to 0.02 mm","Pedal pulsation. Caused by uneven wear and pad deposits."],["Lateral run-out","No more than about 0.05 to 0.10 mm","A warped disc or a hub fault, giving pulsation and noise"],["Surface condition","No deep grooves, no cracks, no heat spots","Deep scoring or cracks mean replacement"]],"caption":"Use the figures in the manual for the vehicle."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Measure thickness with a micrometer at several points around the disc, about 10 mm in from the edge. A disc may be machined on a brake lathe only if the thickness after cutting is still above the minimum. Many modern discs are thin, and are replaced rather than machined."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never drive away until you have pumped the pedal and have a firm pedal. Brake failure after a repair is your responsibility."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Mark the brake hoses and clean the area around the bleed screws and reservoir cap so that no dirt enters the system."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m19-l6", "title": "Brake service in depth: parking brakes, drums and fluid", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Brake service goes beyond pads and discs. Rear brakes with built-in parking mechanisms, electronic parking brakes, drum brakes and the fluid itself all have their own methods. This lesson covers the procedures that are often done incorrectly, and the checks that make a job complete."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Rear calipers with a parking brake"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Many rear calipers have a mechanism for the parking brake inside the piston. The piston is **not simply pushed back**. It must be **wound back** by turning it, with a special tool, as it screws into the caliper. Pushing it back with a clamp breaks the mechanism and the parking brake will not work. Some calipers wind clockwise and some anticlockwise. Look at the manual."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Caliper type","How the piston is retracted"],"rows":[["Front floating caliper, no parking brake","Pushed straight back with a clamp or a tool"],["Rear caliper with a cable parking brake","Wound back with a rotating tool. Check the direction."],["Rear caliper with an electric parking brake motor","Retracted by the motor, using a scan tool or a service mode. Never force it."]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Electronic parking brake (EPB)"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An **electronic parking brake** replaces the handbrake lever with a switch. A small electric motor on each rear caliper presses the pads on to the disc. The control module watches the vehicle, and may apply the brake automatically when you stop, or release it when you pull away. Before you replace pads, you must put the system into **service mode** with the scan tool (or a switch sequence in some models). This releases the pads and retracts the piston, so you can fit the new pads. After the job, you must **close** the service mode and **calibrate** the system, so that it learns the new pad position."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Never push a piston back on an EPB caliper, and never disconnect the motor, unless you are in the correct mode.","Do not use an electronic brake in service mode with the vehicle on a hill.","Check the **parking brake** works after you finish, with the vehicle on a slope, if safe to do so, or on a roller tester.","If the battery is flat, an EPB may be stuck on. The manual gives an emergency release method."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Drum brake service"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Raise and support the vehicle, remove the wheel, and release the parking brake. Loosen the adjuster if the drum is stuck.","Remove the drum. Do not blow the dust out. Clean with a wet method or a vacuum approved for brake dust.","Look at the shoes, springs, wheel cylinder, adjuster and backing plate. Look at the drum bore: measure the diameter against the maximum on the drum.","Note how the springs and the shoes are fitted before you dismantle. Photograph them, or use the second side as a guide.","Replace the shoes in pairs per axle, with new springs and clips if the kit supplies them. Replace a leaking wheel cylinder. Clean and lightly lubricate the contact points of the backing plate with high-temperature brake grease, keeping grease off the linings.","Refit the drum, adjust the shoes by the adjuster or by pumping the brake pedal, and check the parking brake adjustment.","Bleed the brakes if the system was opened."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Brake fluid change in practice"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Method","How","Notes"],"rows":[["Two-person","One presses the pedal and holds, while the other opens and closes the bleed screw","Cheap, needs care to avoid the pedal going too far on an old master cylinder"],["Pressure bleeder","A tank pushes new fluid into the reservoir at low pressure, and the screw is opened at each wheel","Fast and one person. Use the right reservoir cap."],["Vacuum bleeder","A pump draws fluid out through the bleed screw","Can draw air past the screw threads, so seal them with grease"],["Scan-tool-assisted","The tool runs the ABS pump and valves while bleeding","Needed on many ABS systems to clear air from the modulator"]]},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Work from the wheel furthest from the master cylinder to the nearest, unless the manual says otherwise.","Never let the reservoir run dry. Top it up often with the right fluid from a sealed container.","Continue until clean, new fluid with no air bubbles comes out. Close the screw before the pedal is released.","Test the fluid's moisture content before and after, if you have a tester. New fluid has under 1% water.","Dispose of the old fluid as hazardous waste."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Final checks before you hand the car back"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Press the pedal several times. It must be firm, and must not sink.","Check for leaks at every bleed screw, union and caliper.","Check that the wheels turn freely when the parking brake is released, with no dragging.","Tighten the wheel nuts to torque. Re-torque after a short drive.","Road test at low speed first. Check the stop is straight and the pedal firm. Bed in the pads as the manual advises.","Check for warning lights. Clear any codes that came from the work.","Tell the customer to avoid heavy braking for the first 200 km, and that noise may happen as the pads bed in."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Rear parking-brake calipers are wound back and electronic ones are reset with a scan tool. Forcing either one breaks it."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never leave a vehicle with brake work half finished, and never allow a vehicle to leave the workshop until the brakes have been tested and the pedal is firm."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m19-l7", "title": "ABS and stability control", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"When the driver brakes very hard on a slippery surface, the wheels can lock. A locked wheel cannot steer. Anti-lock braking (ABS) prevents this."},
      {"t":"h","text":"How ABS works"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Wheel-speed sensors:** at each wheel, they read the speed from a toothed ring or magnetic ring on the hub or driveshaft.","**ECU:** compares the wheel speeds. If one wheel slows too fast, it is about to lock.","**Hydraulic modulator:** a block of valves and a pump. It quickly holds, reduces and restores the pressure to the wheel that is locking, many times a second.","**Pedal feel:** the driver feels a pulsing in the pedal and may hear a buzz. This is normal during ABS action."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"ABS helps the driver keep the steering. It does not always shorten the stopping distance, and on loose gravel or deep snow it can even lengthen it. The driver should press hard and keep the pedal pressed."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Traction and stability control"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The same sensors and the hydraulic unit help other systems. **Traction control (TCS)** brakes a spinning wheel and reduces engine power. **Electronic stability control (ESC)** uses a steering angle sensor and a yaw (turning) sensor, and brakes individual wheels to correct a skid."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Service and diagnosis"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["If the **ABS light** is on, the ABS is switched off but normal brakes still work. Read the codes with a scan tool.","Common causes: a dirty or damaged wheel-speed sensor, a damaged ring, a broken wire, low battery voltage and low fluid.","Wheel-speed sensors are fragile. Do not hammer them. Clean the ring and the sensor and check the air gap, as the manual says.","Some ABS units need a special bleeding procedure with a scan tool, which opens the valves. Follow the manual."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"If the **red brake warning light** is on at the same time, the problem may be low fluid or a hydraulic fault. This is more serious, and the car should not be driven until it is checked."},
      {"t":"example","title":"What is wheel slip?","steps":["A vehicle is travelling at 20 m/s. During hard braking, a wheel turns so that its rim speed is 16 m/s.","Slip = (vehicle speed − wheel speed) ÷ vehicle speed = (20 − 16) ÷ 20 = 0.20, or 20%.","A tyre gives its highest braking force at a slip of about 10 to 20%. At 100% slip, the wheel is locked and the braking force is less, and steering is lost. ABS keeps the slip in the best range by releasing and reapplying pressure several times a second."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A lit ABS light means the normal brakes are still working but there is no anti-lock protection. A lit red brake light means stop and check."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Look at the live wheel-speed data with a scan tool and spin each wheel by hand to find a dead sensor quickly."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m19-l8", "title": "ABS, stability control and electronic brake systems in depth", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Modern brakes are not just hydraulic. They are controlled by a computer that can apply and release each brake independently, hundreds of times a minute. This lesson looks inside the ABS and the stability control, and shows how to test the sensors, the modulator and the signals."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Wheel speed sensors"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Type","Signal","Notes"],"rows":[["Passive (inductive)","A small AC voltage that rises with wheel speed. A steel toothed ring (tone ring) turns past a magnet and coil.","Two wires. Does not work at very low speed. Resistance of about 1 kΩ."],["Active (Hall or magneto-resistive)","A digital square wave or a current pulse, powered from the ABS module. A magnetic encoder ring or a multi-pole ring turns past the sensor.","Two or three wires. Works down to zero speed, so it can detect a car rolling back."]]},
      {"t":"ul","items":["The **air gap** between the sensor and the ring is small, often 0.5 to 1.5 mm. A dirty ring or a damaged tooth causes errors.","A broken wire, a corroded connector or a damaged ring causes an ABS fault at one wheel.","Metal particles on a magnetic sensor, or a cracked ring in a hub bearing, cause a signal drop-out.","A wheel bearing with play changes the gap and can cause an intermittent fault."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"The hydraulic modulator"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The modulator is a block of valves and a pump. For each wheel, it has an **inlet valve** and an **outlet valve**. In the three phases of an ABS cycle, the module controls these."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Phase","Inlet valve","Outlet valve","Effect at the wheel"],"rows":[["Apply (normal braking)","Open","Closed","Pressure rises with the pedal"],["Hold","Closed","Closed","Pressure is held at a constant value"],["Release","Closed","Open","Pressure is reduced, fluid goes to an accumulator, the pump returns it"]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"A wheel that is about to lock slows down faster than the others. The module goes to release, the wheel speeds up, then it applies again, and so on, 5 to 15 times a second. The driver feels pulsing and may hear the pump and valves. This is normal. The pedal should not go soft, and the car should keep steering."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Stability control (ESC)"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Electronic stability control adds sensors that tell the module where the driver wants to go and where the car is actually going. It then brakes individual wheels and reduces engine power to correct a skid."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Sensor","What it measures"],"rows":[["Steering angle sensor","Where the driver is steering"],["Yaw rate sensor","How fast the car is rotating about its vertical axis"],["Lateral acceleration sensor","Sideways force"],["Wheel speed sensors","The speed of each wheel"],["Brake pressure sensor","What the driver is asking for"]]},
      {"t":"example","title":"How ESC corrects an oversteer","steps":["The driver steers left, but the yaw rate shows the car rotating faster than the steering and speed say it should: the tail is sliding out.","The module applies the brake on the front outside wheel (the right front), which makes a force that rotates the car back.","At the same time it reduces engine torque. The correction takes a fraction of a second, and the driver may only feel a light pulse and see a flashing lamp."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Other functions that use the same hardware"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Traction control:** brakes a spinning wheel and reduces engine power.","**Brake assist:** detects a panic stop from the speed of the pedal movement and applies full pressure at once.","**Hill-start assist:** holds the brakes for a moment when you move from the brake to the accelerator on a slope.","**Hill descent control:** applies the brakes to hold a low speed on a steep descent.","**Automatic emergency braking:** uses radar and camera signals to brake without the driver (module 12).","**Brake-by-wire and regenerative blending:** in electric and hybrid cars, the pedal is a sensor and the system blends motor and friction braking (module 13)."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Diagnosing with a scan tool"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Read the codes in the ABS module and the other modules. Note whether they are current or stored.","Look at the **wheel speeds** in live data while rolling the car slowly, or on a road test. All four should be close together. A wheel that reads zero, or jumps, has a sensor, wiring or ring fault.","Run the **actuator tests** for the pump and valves.","Check the **steering angle** and **yaw rate** values with the car stationary and straight. They should read near zero. A big offset means the sensor needs calibrating.","Check the **brake light switch** signal and the brake pressure sensor.","After repairs, clear codes, and calibrate sensors as needed."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","Likely cause"],"rows":[["ABS light on, normal brakes work","A wheel speed sensor, wiring, ring or module fault, low battery voltage"],["ABS and brake lights on together","A hydraulic fault, low fluid, or a serious module fault. The vehicle may be unsafe."],["ABS cycles on dry roads at low speed","A faulty sensor or a cracked tone ring that gives a false drop in speed"],["Stability control light flashing in a turn","Normal: the system is active"],["Stability control light on constantly","A steering angle or yaw sensor needing calibration, a wheel sensor, or a tyre size mismatch"],["Pedal pulsing in every stop","A disc fault, or a wheel sensor fault making ABS run unnecessarily"]]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"ABS and ESC are only as good as their sensors. Check the sensors, rings, wiring and battery before you replace a modulator."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"After a wheel alignment, a new steering rack or suspension work, always calibrate the steering angle sensor if the maker requires it."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m19-l9", "title": "Brake faults and diagnosis", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Brake complaints must be taken seriously. The rule is simple: **if the brakes are doubtful, the vehicle does not go back on the road.**"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","Likely causes","What to check"],"rows":[["Spongy pedal","Air in the lines, old or boiled fluid, a swollen hose","Fluid condition, bleeding, hoses"],["Pedal goes to the floor slowly","A leak or a failing master cylinder seal","Level, leaks, wheel cylinders and calipers"],["Hard pedal and long stopping","Booster or vacuum fault, contaminated pads, glazed pads","Vacuum hose, booster, pads"],["Pulling to one side","A seized caliper, a blocked hose, uneven pads, a tyre fault","Caliper slides, hoses, pads, tyres"],["Judder or pulsation in the pedal","Disc thickness variation or runout, loose wheel nuts, wheel bearing play","Disc measurements, wheel nut torque"],["Squeal or grind","Worn pads, dry slide pins, glazed pads, metal contact","Pad thickness, disc surface"],["Drag, hot wheel, smell","A seized caliper or piston, a sticky cable, a blocked hose","Wheel temperature, handbrake cable"],["Falling fluid level","A leak or worn pads","Pipes, hoses, cylinders, pad thickness"]],"caption":"Brake fault diagnosis"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Road and bench tests"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Start with the fluid level and a visual inspection of the lines, hoses and wheels. With the engine off, press the pedal several times to remove the vacuum, then hold the pedal and start the engine. The pedal should sink slightly, which shows the booster works. In a safe place, test the car at low speed to see if it stops straight and the pedal is firm. A hot wheel hub after a short drive shows a dragging brake."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Overheating and fade"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Long hard braking, for example on a long descent, overheats the brakes and causes **fade**, when the brakes lose strength. Use a lower gear to let the engine help. Boiled fluid causes a soft pedal that returns when cool, which shows the fluid is old."},
      {"t":"h","text":"A systematic brake inspection"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Look at the **pedal feel** and the travel. Press the pedal firmly and hold it for 30 seconds. It should not sink.","Check the **fluid level and condition**, and the master cylinder for leaks.","Take off the wheels and check each pad, disc, caliper, slide pins, boots and hoses.","Check the **handbrake** adjustment and operation.","Check the **pipes, hoses and unions** for leaks and corrosion along their whole length.","Test the **ABS and warning lights** and read the codes.","Road test: check stopping straight, pedal feel, noise, pull and handbrake hold."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Record the pad thickness, disc thickness and any faults on the job card and give the customer a report. Any brake fault that you find is a safety defect, and should be recorded in writing, even if the customer does not wish to repair it at once."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"If the pedal is soft, sinks, or the car pulls strongly, do not road-test at speed. Tow the vehicle or find and fix the fault first."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Find the cause. A seized caliper is often a result of a collapsed hose or a dry slide pin. Replacing only the caliper may repeat the fault."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m19-l10", "title": "Brake testing, inspection standards and wheel bearings in practice", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A brake that feels fine may still be unsafe, and a brake that feels odd may be within limits. Testing gives measured results that can be compared with a standard. This lesson explains how brakes are tested, how to read the results, and applies the same thinking to wheel bearings and hubs."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The roller brake tester"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **roller brake tester** has a pair of rollers under each axle. The vehicle's wheels sit on them, and the tester turns the wheels while the driver presses the brake pedal. A sensor measures the force at each wheel. It shows the braking force of each wheel, the imbalance between the left and right and the efficiency of the whole system."},
      {"t":"formula","text":"Braking efficiency (%) = total braking force ÷ vehicle weight × 100","caption":"Vehicle weight is in newtons: mass in kilograms times 9.81"},
      {"t":"example","title":"Reading a roller test result","steps":["The measured forces are: front left 2.9 kN, front right 2.8 kN, rear left 1.6 kN, rear right 1.5 kN. The total is 8.8 kN.","The vehicle's mass is 1,350 kg, so its weight is 1,350 × 9.81 = 13.24 kN.","Efficiency = 8.8 ÷ 13.24 × 100 = 66%. A common legal minimum for the service brake is about 50%, so this passes.","Front imbalance = (2.9 − 2.8) ÷ 2.9 = 3.4%. A typical limit is about 25 to 30%, so this is acceptable. A larger difference means one side is not working properly.","The parking brake, tested on the rear axle alone at 1.0 kN, gives 1.0 ÷ 13.24 = 7.6%, against a minimum of about 16%. This fails. The parking brake needs adjustment or repair."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Test result","What it shows","Next step"],"rows":[["Low force on one wheel","A seized caliper, contaminated pad, collapsed hose or a leaking wheel cylinder","Inspect that wheel"],["Both rear wheels low","Proportioning valve, worn rear pads, or a faulty ABS rear control","Check the valve and the pads"],["Force rising and falling as the wheel turns","Disc thickness variation or run-out, or an out-of-round drum","Measure the disc or drum"],["A wheel locks early","Overworking that wheel, or a fault on the other side","Compare the left and right"],["Drag shown with the pedal released","A sticking caliper, a seized handbrake cable or a faulty return spring","Free the part"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"A road test for brakes"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Test in a safe, empty place on a dry road. First press the pedal at low speed to check it is working.","At about 30 to 50 km/h, brake firmly and look for a pull, pedal pulsing, noise and the stopping distance.","Test the **parking brake** on a slope or by trying to drive against it.","Check that the **ABS** works only in a safe place, on a wet or loose surface.","Do not do a hard brake test with the vehicle if the brakes are suspect. Use a roller tester first."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Wheel bearings and hubs: further checks"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Test","How","What it shows"],"rows":[["Rock test","With the wheel raised, hold it at 12 and 6 o'clock and rock it. Then at 3 and 9 o'clock.","Play in the bearing, the ball joints or the tie-rod ends"],["Spin test","Spin the wheel by hand, with the brake released, and listen and feel for roughness","A rough or noisy bearing"],["Road test, turn left and right","Drive in gentle S-turns at 50 to 70 km/h and listen for a hum that changes","A hum that is louder when turning right points to the left bearing, since load moves to that side, and the reverse"],["Dial gauge test","Measure the axial play at the hub face","Compare with the limit, often under 0.05 mm for a hub unit"],["ABS signal","Look at the wheel speed signal with a scope or the scan tool","Drop-outs from a worn bearing or a damaged encoder"]]},
      {"t":"example","title":"Which bearing is noisy?","steps":["A car hums at 80 km/h. The hum gets louder in a long left curve and quieter in a right curve.","In a left curve the weight transfers to the right side, so the bearings on the **right** carry more load. The louder hum points to the right bearing.","Confirm by lifting the wheel and spinning and rocking it. A roughness or play confirms it. Replace the bearing and test again."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Reporting brake and bearing findings"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Write the exact measurements: pad thicknesses, disc thicknesses, the play in the bearing and the roller test results. If a part is below the limit, mark it as unsafe and tell the customer in writing, in clear words. Never sign a vehicle as safe if any brake component is below its limit, if there is a fluid leak, or if the roller test fails."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Numbers beat feelings. Measure thickness, run-out, force and play, and compare each with the limit."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Stay clear of the rollers and of the front of the vehicle during a roller test, and never stand behind a wheel that is being driven. Chock the wheels and use the correct tester procedure."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m19-l11", "title": "Hubs and wheel bearings", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Wheel bearings let the wheels turn with little friction while carrying the weight of the vehicle. A failing bearing makes noise and, if ignored, can cause a wheel to lock or come off."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Bearing types"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Tapered roller bearings:** used in pairs, with a preload set by a nut. Older vehicles and many trucks use them. They need correct adjustment and grease.","**Sealed hub-bearing units (cartridge):** a sealed double-row ball bearing in a unit, standard on many modern cars. It is replaced as a whole and is not adjusted.","**Floating axle or press-fit bearings:** in solid rear axles, the bearing can be pressed on an axle shaft."]},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"wheel-bearing"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Symptoms and checks"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The common sign is a **growl or hum** that changes with speed and gets louder when turning to one side, because the weight moves over the bearing. To check, raise the wheel and spin it by hand to feel roughness and listen for noise. Hold the wheel at the top and bottom and rock it to find **play**. A loose bearing gives play and uneven pad wear and may upset the ABS signal."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Replacement"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Support the vehicle safely and remove the wheel, caliper and disc.","Release the **driveshaft nut** where there is one, which is tight. Use the correct socket and follow the manual, as it is often a single-use nut.","Remove the hub unit bolts and the unit, and clean the knuckle seat.","Fit the new hub, taking care of the ABS ring or sensor.","Tighten all the bolts and the driveshaft nut to the specified torque, and fit the new retaining pin or lock if needed."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Torque is critical, because too tight overheats the bearing and too loose lets it move. Do not tighten a hub-bearing unit by hitting it. Use the proper tool or a press."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Tyres in brief"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Worn, damaged or wrongly inflated tyres reduce braking a great deal. Check the tread, pressure and sidewalls whenever you are working on the brakes. You can find more on tyre markings in module 3."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Choosing and fitting a hub bearing"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Fit the correct unit for the vehicle's model, year and brake type. Hub bearing units for wheels with and without ABS look alike but differ.","Clean the knuckle bore, and remove burrs and rust, so the new bearing sits square. A rusty bore stops the bearing seating properly.","Press with the correct tool on the correct ring. Never hammer on a bearing.","Tighten the axle nut to the specified torque, and fit a new retaining pin or use a new locking nut if specified. The torque is very high, often 200 to 300 N·m on a front drive axle.","Refit the ABS sensor and check the air gap and the wheel-speed signal."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"A failing wheel bearing is a serious risk. If a customer reports a loud growl, check it before the vehicle leaves."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Always use the correct torque on the driveshaft nut and the hub bolts. These parts carry the whole weight of the vehicle."}
    ]}
  ],
  "practical": {"title":"Inspect and service brakes, bleed and test","objective":"Inspect and measure pads, discs and drums, bleed a brake system correctly on a training vehicle and test a brake after service.","time":"120 minutes","tools":["Training vehicle or brake rig on a lift or stands","Wheel brace, torque wrench and socket set","Vernier caliper or micrometer, and a dial gauge for disc runout","Brake piston tool, brake cleaner, brake grease and clean rags","Brake fluid of the specified grade, bleed hose and a clear container","Dust mask, gloves and eye protection, and the workshop manual"],"safety":["Support the vehicle on a lift or stands, never on a jack alone, and chock the wheels.","Wear a dust mask and do not blow brake dust with compressed air.","Brake fluid damages paint and the skin, so wipe spills and wash your hands.","Never let a caliper hang on its hose.","Do not allow any grease on the pads, the discs or the drums.","Do not drive the vehicle until the pedal is firm."],"steps":["Read the pad, disc and torque figures in the manual and write them down.","Check the fluid level and condition and look for leaks at all hoses and pipes.","Remove a wheel and measure the pad thickness on both sides of the caliper.","Measure the disc thickness in several places and check the runout with a dial gauge.","Remove a drum if the vehicle has one and measure the diameter and the shoe lining.","Check the caliper slide pins and the boots, and clean and lubricate them.","Fit the pads, push the piston back and refit the caliper to the specified torque.","Refit the wheel and tighten the nuts to the specified torque in a star pattern.","Bleed the brakes in the correct order, keeping the reservoir topped up.","Pump the pedal until it is firm. Check for leaks and re-check the fluid level.","Make a low-speed brake test in a safe area and note the result.","Write a record of measurements, the work done and the test result."],"checks":["I recorded the pad, disc and drum measurements against the limits.","I lubricated the correct parts and kept the friction surfaces clean.","I bled the brakes in the correct order without letting the reservoir empty.","I tested the brake before releasing the vehicle.","I wore a dust mask and worked safely."]},
  "quiz": [
    {"id":"m19-q01","q":"Why does a hydraulic brake system work well?","options":["The pedal is joined to the pads by a long steel rod","The engine drives a pump that presses the pads at all times","Air in the pipes pushes the pads to the disc by its own pressure","Liquids cannot be compressed, so pressure moves through the pipes to the brakes"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m19-l1","explain":"Pressure is the same in all parts of a closed system of liquid, and the piston area gives the force."},
    {"id":"m19-q02","q":"Why does a master cylinder have two circuits?","options":["If one circuit fails, the other can still brake the vehicle","So the pedal can be pressed twice as far as before","So the front and rear pads wear at exactly the same rate","So the brake fluid can be heated in one of the two circuits for a better pedal feel"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l1","explain":"A tandem master cylinder gives a safe back-up if one circuit leaks."},
    {"id":"m19-q03","q":"What does a brake booster do?","options":["It adds force so the pedal is light to press","It stores the brake fluid for the master cylinder","It cools the brake pads when the vehicle is moving","It stops the wheels from locking in a hard stop"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m19-l1","explain":"A vacuum booster uses engine vacuum, and diesel and electric vehicles use a pump."},
    {"id":"m19-q04","q":"Why does brake fluid need to be changed on schedule?","options":["It becomes thicker with age and then blocks the pipes completely","It absorbs water, which lowers its boiling point and causes corrosion","It evaporates from the reservoir through the cap quickly","It loses its colour and no longer lubricates the pads"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m19-l1","explain":"Boiled wet fluid makes vapour bubbles that compress, and the pedal goes to the floor."},
    {"id":"m19-q05","q":"Which brake fluid must never be mixed with glycol-based fluids?","options":["DOT 3 fluid from a sealed can","DOT 5.1 fluid from a sealed can","DOT 4 fluid from a sealed can","DOT 5 silicone fluid"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l1","explain":"DOT 5 is silicone based and is not compatible with glycol types. Use only the fluid in the manual."},
    {"id":"m19-q06","q":"A small master cylinder piston and a big caliper piston are joined by the same fluid. What is the result?","options":["The big piston gives a bigger force from the same pressure","The big piston gives a smaller force at the same pressure","The pressure is higher at the big piston than at the small one","The big piston moves further than the small piston does"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l1","explain":"Force equals pressure times area, so a bigger area gives a bigger force."},
    {"id":"m19-q07","q":"A car travels at 27.8 m/s on a dry road with μ = 0.8. What is the braking distance?","options":["About 12 m","About 49 m","About 150 m","About 5 m"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l2","explain":"Distance = v² ÷ (2 μ g) = 772.8 ÷ 15.7 = 49.2 m."},
    {"id":"m19-q08","q":"If the speed doubles, what happens to the braking distance?","options":["It doubles as well","It stays the same as before","It becomes four times as long","It halves, as the car is quicker"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l2","explain":"Braking distance depends on the square of the speed, so doubling the speed gives four times the distance."},
    {"id":"m19-q09","q":"On a wet road μ is 0.5. What is the braking distance from 27.8 m/s?","options":["About 49 m","About 20 m","About 150 m","About 79 m"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m19-l2","explain":"Distance = 772.8 ÷ (2 × 0.5 × 9.81) = 772.8 ÷ 9.81 = 78.8 m. About 30 m longer than on a dry road."},
    {"id":"m19-q10","q":"Why do front brakes do more work than rear brakes?","options":["Weight moves forward","They are always made of better steel","They are nearer the driver's seat","They carry the fuel tank"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l2","explain":"Under braking, the front tyres carry more load and can give more braking force."},
    {"id":"m19-q11","q":"A car of 1,400 kg brakes at 0.8 g, with a CG height of 0.55 m and a wheelbase of 2.6 m. What is the weight transfer?","options":["About 230 N, or 24 kg","About 23,000 N, or 2,370 kg","About 2,300 N","About 23 N, or 2.4 kg"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m19-l2","explain":"Transfer = 1,400 × 7.85 × 0.55 ÷ 2.6 = 2,325 N, which is about 237 kg moved to the front."},
    {"id":"m19-q12","q":"What is brake fade?","options":["Loss of braking from heat","A change in the colour of the discs","A noise when the pads are new","A warning light on the dashboard"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m19-l2","explain":"Heat reduces the friction of pads or boils the fluid, so the brakes get weaker, with a hard or soft pedal."},
    {"id":"m19-q13","q":"What does a floating caliper slide on?","options":["The brake disc, which must be coated with grease","Slide pins, which must be clean and lubricated","The brake pads, which must be free from any grease","The wheel hub, which must be kept dry and clean"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m19-l3","explain":"Seized slide pins cause uneven pad wear, drag and pulling."},
    {"id":"m19-q14","q":"Why are discs better than drums at cooling?","options":["They are enclosed so heat is kept inside the brake","They are made of thinner metal that never gets hot","They have return springs that carry heat away","They are open to the air, so heat leaves faster"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l3","explain":"Drums enclose the shoes, so heat is not so easy to remove."},
    {"id":"m19-q15","q":"What is the job of the automatic adjuster in a drum brake?","options":["To keep the clearance between the shoes and the drum small as the lining wears","To measure the thickness of the lining and warn the driver","To pull the shoes away from the drum each time the pedal is released","To lock the wheel when the handbrake is pulled up"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m19-l3","explain":"Without it, the pedal travel grows as the lining wears."},
    {"id":"m19-q16","q":"A grinding noise comes from a front brake. What does it usually show?","options":["The pad material is gone and metal is rubbing on the disc","The tyres are inflated to the wrong pressure for the load","The brake fluid is too new and has not yet settled in","The wheel bearing has been greased too much"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l3","explain":"Stop driving the vehicle, because the disc is being damaged and braking is poor."},
    {"id":"m19-q17","q":"What is the correct way to treat brake dust?","options":["Blow it away with an air line to clean the area quickly","Leave it on the parts because it protects the metal","Use brake cleaner or a damp cloth and wear a dust mask","Brush it with a dry brush and breathe normally"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m19-l3","explain":"Old brake dust can contain harmful fibres, so do not raise it into the air."},
    {"id":"m19-q18","q":"What causes a high squeal when braking lightly?","options":["A low washer fluid level in the bottle","A worn tyre on the same corner","Pad vibration","A flat battery in the engine bay"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l3","explain":"The pad vibrates against the disc. Clean the slides, lubricate them with brake grease and replace worn shims."},
    {"id":"m19-q19","q":"A driver pushes with 150 N on a pedal of ratio 4:1. What force is at the pushrod?","options":["37.5 N","150 N","6,000 N","600 N"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l4","explain":"Force at the pushrod = pedal force times lever ratio, so 150 N times 4 gives 600 N."},
    {"id":"m19-q20","q":"A force of 2,400 N acts on a master cylinder of 445 mm². What is the pressure?","options":["About 0.19 N/mm², or 1.9 bar","About 5.4 N/mm², or 54 bar","About 54 N/mm², or 540 bar","About 540 bar, or 5.4 N/mm²"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m19-l4","explain":"Pressure = force ÷ area = 2,400 ÷ 445 = 5.39 N/mm². 1 N/mm² is 10 bar, so 54 bar."},
    {"id":"m19-q21","q":"What does a servo (booster) do?","options":["It cools the discs while braking","It measures the wheel speeds","It supplies the brake fluid","It multiplies pedal force"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l4","explain":"A vacuum servo adds a large force to the driver's push. If it fails, the brakes still work but the pedal is hard."},
    {"id":"m19-q22","q":"Why does air in the fluid give a spongy pedal?","options":["Air makes the fluid thicker","Air compresses","Air makes the pads wear faster","Air lowers the fluid temperature"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l4","explain":"Fluid cannot be compressed but air can. Part of the pedal movement goes to squeezing the bubble."},
    {"id":"m19-q23","q":"The pedal sinks slowly when held firmly for 30 seconds. What does this suggest?","options":["A leak or a bad seal","A good, healthy brake system","A worn drive belt on the engine","A blown fuse in the cabin"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l4","explain":"A brake pedal that sinks under steady pressure shows fluid is escaping or bypassing a seal. It is unsafe."},
    {"id":"m19-q24","q":"When changing pads, where should the caliper be hung?","options":["On the brake hose, to keep it close to the bracket","From the spring or a hook, never on the brake hose","On the disc, resting on the top edge","On the wheel stud, with the hose pulled tight"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l5","explain":"A hose carrying the weight of the caliper can be damaged inside."},
    {"id":"m19-q25","q":"What should you watch when pushing a caliper piston back?","options":["The handbrake cable, because it releases when the piston moves","The brake fluid level, because it rises in the reservoir","The tyre pressure, because it rises when the piston moves","The wheel nut torque, because it falls when the piston moves"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m19-l5","explain":"Remove some fluid if the reservoir is full, and never use old fluid."},
    {"id":"m19-q26","q":"What may be lubricated with brake grease?","options":["The disc surface, to protect it from rust","The faces of the new pads, to make them quiet","The caliper slide pins and contact points, never the friction surfaces","The inside of the drum, to stop the shoes squeaking"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m19-l5","explain":"Grease on the friction surfaces causes brake failure."},
    {"id":"m19-q27","q":"In which order do you usually bleed the brakes?","options":["Start with the wheel that has the newest pads","Start with the rear wheel on the driver's side every time","Start with the wheel nearest to the master cylinder always","Start with the wheel furthest from the master cylinder, unless the manual says otherwise"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l5","explain":"The manual gives the exact order for the vehicle, which can differ with the circuit layout."},
    {"id":"m19-q28","q":"What is the reason for bedding in new pads?","options":["To form an even layer of friction material on the disc and give full braking power","To make the brake fluid warm up and flow better","To remove the grease that the factory put on the pads","To make the wheel nuts tighten themselves in use"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l5","explain":"Use moderate braking over the first few hundred kilometres. Avoid hard stops if you can."},
    {"id":"m19-q29","q":"What must you do before driving the vehicle after brake work?","options":["Drive at speed on the road to bed the new pads in quickly","Wash the wheels and then drive off to the customer","Pump the pedal until it is firm and check for leaks","Start the engine and drive off with the first press of the pedal"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m19-l5","explain":"The pistons must be moved out to the pads. The first press may go almost to the floor if you forget."},
    {"id":"m19-q30","q":"A disc has a minimum thickness stamped at 22.0 mm and it measures 21.8 mm. What should you do?","options":["Machine it","Replace it","Refit it","Paint it"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l5","explain":"A disc below its minimum thickness overheats and can crack. It must be replaced, not machined."},
    {"id":"m19-q31","q":"How is the piston of a rear caliper with a cable parking brake retracted?","options":["Push it with a large clamp","Hit it with a soft hammer","Wind it back with a tool","Cut the parking brake cable"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l6","explain":"The parking brake mechanism is inside the piston. Pushing it straight back breaks the mechanism."},
    {"id":"m19-q32","q":"What must be done before replacing pads on an electronic parking brake caliper?","options":["Disconnect the motor and pull the wires out","Use the scan tool service mode","Push the piston back with force","Drain all the fluid from the system"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l6","explain":"Service mode retracts the pads safely and allows the job. After the job, the mode is closed and calibrated."},
    {"id":"m19-q33","q":"Which wheel is bled first in a normal bleeding procedure?","options":["The nearest wheel to the master cylinder","The driver's front wheel always","The furthest wheel","The spare wheel in the boot"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m19-l6","explain":"Unless the manual says otherwise, bleed from the furthest to the nearest wheel."},
    {"id":"m19-q34","q":"Why must the master cylinder reservoir never run dry during bleeding?","options":["Air would get in","The brake lights would not work at all","The disc would overheat quickly","The wheel would lock on its own"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m19-l6","explain":"Air in the master cylinder means the whole system must be bled again, and takes much longer."},
    {"id":"m19-q35","q":"What does ABS prevent?","options":["Any skidding at all, on any road surface and at any speed","The need to press the pedal firmly in an emergency","The brake pads from wearing out in normal use","Wheel lock-up in hard braking, so the driver can still steer"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l7","explain":"ABS keeps the wheels turning, which keeps some grip and steering."},
    {"id":"m19-q36","q":"Which part tells the ABS ECU how fast each wheel is turning?","options":["The master cylinder reservoir float switch","The brake pad wear sensor on the caliper","The wheel-speed sensor","The brake pedal position switch on the pedal"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m19-l7","explain":"A toothed or magnetic ring on the hub gives a signal to the sensor."},
    {"id":"m19-q37","q":"What does pulsing in the pedal during a hard stop on a wet road usually mean?","options":["The brake fluid is boiling in the hoses","The master cylinder has failed","The disc is warped and must be replaced at once","The ABS is working"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l7","explain":"A pulse and a buzz are a normal sign of ABS action."},
    {"id":"m19-q38","q":"The ABS light is on, and the red brake light is off. What does it mean?","options":["The pads are worn out and must be replaced","The normal brakes still work, but there is no anti-lock protection","The brakes have failed completely, and the car must be towed","The brake fluid is too low in both of the circuits"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m19-l7","explain":"Read the codes. A dirty sensor or a broken wire is common."},
    {"id":"m19-q39","q":"What does electronic stability control (ESC) use to detect a skid?","options":["The oil pressure and the coolant temperature","Only the pedal switch and the handbrake switch","A steering angle sensor and a yaw sensor, with the wheel-speed sensors","The tyre pressure sensors in the four wheels"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l7","explain":"It brakes single wheels to bring the car back to the driver's line."},
    {"id":"m19-q40","q":"A tyre gives the best braking force at about what slip?","options":["About 0%","About 100%","About 50%","About 10 to 20%"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l8","explain":"The braking force peaks at a small slip. A locked wheel (100% slip) gives less force and no steering."},
    {"id":"m19-q41","q":"A vehicle is at 20 m/s and a wheel has a rim speed of 16 m/s. What is the slip?","options":["80%","4%","20%","0.2%"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m19-l8","explain":"Slip = (20 − 16) ÷ 20 = 0.20, which is 20%."},
    {"id":"m19-q42","q":"In the release phase of ABS, what are the two valves doing?","options":["Both valves are open together","Inlet open and outlet shut","Both valves are shut tight","Inlet shut, outlet open"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l8","explain":"To reduce pressure, the inlet valve closes to cut off the master cylinder, and the outlet opens to release fluid."},
    {"id":"m19-q43","q":"Which sensor tells ESC how fast the car is rotating?","options":["The yaw rate sensor","The coolant sensor","The oil pressure switch","The fuel gauge sender"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m19-l8","explain":"Yaw rate shows rotation about the vertical axis. It is compared with what the steering angle says the driver wants."},
    {"id":"m19-q44","q":"An ABS light is on but the normal brakes work. What is a likely cause?","options":["A completely empty brake fluid reservoir","A sensor, ring or wiring fault","A seized piston in a caliper","A broken brake pedal arm"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l8","explain":"Most ABS faults are sensor-related. If the brake light is also on, it may be hydraulic and unsafe."},
    {"id":"m19-q45","q":"A pedal feels spongy. What is the most likely cause?","options":["A cracked exhaust manifold that is leaking hot gas near the engine","Air in the brake lines or old, wet fluid","Worn wiper blades on the windscreen","A discharged battery"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m19-l9","explain":"Air can be compressed, so the pedal feels soft. Bleed with new fluid."},
    {"id":"m19-q46","q":"The pedal is hard and the car needs a long distance to stop. What should you check?","options":["The wiper motor and the washer pump","The coolant level in the expansion tank","The booster and its vacuum supply","The air-conditioning belt and the idler pulley"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m19-l9","explain":"With the engine off, pump the pedal then hold it and start the engine. The pedal should sink a little."},
    {"id":"m19-q47","q":"A car pulls to the left when braking. What are common causes?","options":["A seized caliper or uneven pads or tyres on one side","A low level of washer fluid in the reservoir under the bonnet","A faulty horn relay in the fuse box","A loose cap on the fuel tank"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l9","explain":"Check both sides and the slides, hoses and tyres. Always repair brakes in axle sets."},
    {"id":"m19-q48","q":"A pedal pulsation is felt at all speeds on light braking. What is the most likely cause?","options":["A fault in the engine oil pressure switch","An overfilled windscreen washer bottle","Disc thickness variation or runout, or a loose wheel","A worn spark plug in one of the cylinders"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l9","explain":"Measure the discs with a micrometer and a dial gauge, and check the wheel nuts and the bearings."},
    {"id":"m19-q49","q":"A customer says the brake pedal went soft after a long mountain descent but is now normal. What is the most likely cause?","options":["A fault in the fuel pump relay in the fuse box","A blocked air filter that reduced engine power","Brake fade from heat, or boiling of old fluid","A leak in the exhaust system near the catalyst"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m19-l9","explain":"Check the fluid age, and advise using a lower gear on descents."},
    {"id":"m19-q50","q":"What do you do if the brakes are doubtful and the customer wants the car back?","options":["Release the car because they are in a hurry to leave","Release the car and tell them to brake gently on the road","Tell them to come back when the pedal is completely on the floor","Do not release it. Explain the fault and fix it first"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l9","explain":"A vehicle with doubtful brakes does not go back on the road."},
    {"id":"m19-q51","q":"Total roller test force is 8.8 kN and the weight is 13.24 kN. What is the efficiency?","options":["About 66%","About 15%","About 150%","About 6.6%"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m19-l10","explain":"Efficiency = 8.8 ÷ 13.24 × 100 = 66%. A typical minimum for the service brake is about 50%."},
    {"id":"m19-q52","q":"A front axle roller test gives 2.9 kN and 2.8 kN. Is the imbalance acceptable?","options":["No, it is 30%","Yes, it is about 3%","No, it is 100%","It cannot be calculated"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l10","explain":"The difference is 0.1 kN out of 2.9 kN, about 3.4%. A typical limit is about 25 to 30%."},
    {"id":"m19-q53","q":"A car hums louder in a left curve. Which wheel bearing is likely at fault?","options":["The left bearing on the same axle","The rear bearing of the spare","The spare wheel in the boot","The right bearing"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l10","explain":"In a left turn the weight moves to the right side. A noisy bearing under more load is louder."},
    {"id":"m19-q54","q":"What must you do if any brake component is below its limit?","options":["Say nothing and return the vehicle","Paint the worn part to hide it","Tell the customer in writing","Sign the vehicle off as safe"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m19-l10","explain":"Unsafe brakes must be reported clearly, with the measurements, and recorded on the job card."},
    {"id":"m19-q55","q":"What noise often shows a failing wheel bearing?","options":["A growl or hum that changes with speed","A tick that stops when the engine warms up","A hiss when the car is stopped","A short squeal when the brake pedal is pressed"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m19-l11","explain":"It can be louder when turning to one side, as the weight moves over the bearing."},
    {"id":"m19-q56","q":"What type of bearing does a sealed hub unit contain?","options":["A plain bush made of rubber","A sealed double-row ball bearing, replaced as a whole unit","A needle bearing that must be greased every month","A tapered roller bearing adjusted with a castle nut"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l11","explain":"It is not adjustable. If the bearing is faulty, replace the unit."},
    {"id":"m19-q57","q":"Why is the torque on the hub nut and bolts so important?","options":["It only matters for the appearance of the nut and washer","Too tight overheats the bearing and too loose lets it move","Too tight makes the brake noisy and too loose makes it quiet","It has no effect if a locking pin is fitted"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m19-l11","explain":"Use a torque wrench and the figure in the manual. Use new single-use nuts where required."},
    {"id":"m19-q58","q":"Where should hub bearing press force be applied?","options":["On the balls only","On the cage of the bearing","On the grease seal","On the tight ring"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m19-l11","explain":"Press through the ring that is tight in its housing. Never force through the rolling elements, which damages them."}
  ],
  "cards": [
    {"id":"m19-c01","front":"Why does hydraulic braking work?","back":"Liquid cannot be compressed, so pressure moves through the pipes to the brakes.","lesson":"m19-l1"},
    {"id":"m19-c02","front":"Why is a master cylinder tandem?","back":"It has two circuits so one can still work if the other fails.","lesson":"m19-l1"},
    {"id":"m19-c03","front":"What does a booster do?","back":"It adds force so the pedal is light.","lesson":"m19-l1"},
    {"id":"m19-c04","front":"What does brake fluid absorb?","back":"Water from the air, which lowers its boiling point.","lesson":"m19-l1"},
    {"id":"m19-c05","front":"Which DOT fluid is silicone based?","back":"DOT 5. Never mix it with glycol fluids.","lesson":"m19-l1"},
    {"id":"m19-c06","front":"What must be used for brake pipes?","back":"Steel or copper-nickel pipe of the right size and flare. Never plumbing copper or plastic.","lesson":"m19-l1"},
    {"id":"m19-c07","front":"Braking distance formula?","back":"v² ÷ (2 × μ × g). At 27.8 m/s with μ = 0.8 it is about 49 m.","lesson":"m19-l2"},
    {"id":"m19-c08","front":"Typical friction coefficients?","back":"About 0.8 on dry asphalt, 0.5 on wet, 0.1 on ice.","lesson":"m19-l2"},
    {"id":"m19-c09","front":"Why does speed matter so much?","back":"Braking distance grows with the square of the speed. Double the speed, four times the distance.","lesson":"m19-l2"},
    {"id":"m19-c10","front":"What is weight transfer when braking?","back":"Weight moves to the front. Mass × deceleration × CG height ÷ wheelbase.","lesson":"m19-l2"},
    {"id":"m19-c11","front":"What is a floating caliper?","back":"A caliper that slides on pins and has a piston on one side.","lesson":"m19-l3"},
    {"id":"m19-c12","front":"What does a drum brake adjuster do?","back":"Keeps the shoe clearance small as the lining wears.","lesson":"m19-l3"},
    {"id":"m19-c13","front":"What does a grinding brake noise show?","back":"Metal is rubbing on metal, because the pad material is gone.","lesson":"m19-l3"},
    {"id":"m19-c14","front":"How do you clean brake dust?","back":"With brake cleaner or a damp cloth, wearing a mask.","lesson":"m19-l3"},
    {"id":"m19-c15","front":"How is pedal force multiplied?","back":"By the pedal lever, the servo, and the hydraulic area ratio between master cylinder and caliper.","lesson":"m19-l4"},
    {"id":"m19-c16","front":"Pressure formula?","back":"Pressure = force ÷ area. 1 N/mm² is 10 bar.","lesson":"m19-l4"},
    {"id":"m19-c17","front":"Why does air cause a spongy pedal?","back":"Air compresses, so pedal travel is used up before pressure builds.","lesson":"m19-l4"},
    {"id":"m19-c18","front":"How do you hang a caliper?","back":"From a hook or spring, never on the hose.","lesson":"m19-l5"},
    {"id":"m19-c19","front":"What do you lubricate with brake grease?","back":"The slide pins and contact points, never the friction surfaces.","lesson":"m19-l5"},
    {"id":"m19-c20","front":"Where do you start bleeding?","back":"At the wheel furthest from the master cylinder, unless the manual says otherwise.","lesson":"m19-l5"},
    {"id":"m19-c21","front":"What must you do after brake work?","back":"Pump the pedal until firm and test at low speed.","lesson":"m19-l5"},
    {"id":"m19-c22","front":"Why bed in new pads?","back":"To form an even layer of friction material for full power.","lesson":"m19-l5"},
    {"id":"m19-c23","front":"Disc checks?","back":"Thickness against the stamped minimum, thickness variation, run-out and surface condition.","lesson":"m19-l5"},
    {"id":"m19-c24","front":"How is a rear caliper with a parking brake reset?","back":"Wind the piston back with a rotating tool. Do not push it.","lesson":"m19-l6"},
    {"id":"m19-c25","front":"What must be done on an EPB before a pad change?","back":"Use the scan tool service mode, then close and calibrate after.","lesson":"m19-l6"},
    {"id":"m19-c26","front":"Which wheel is bled first?","back":"At the wheel furthest from the master cylinder, unless the manual says otherwise.","lesson":"m19-l6"},
    {"id":"m19-c27","front":"What does ABS prevent?","back":"Wheel lock-up, so the driver can still steer.","lesson":"m19-l7"},
    {"id":"m19-c28","front":"What reads wheel speed for ABS?","back":"The wheel-speed sensors, at a toothed or magnetic ring.","lesson":"m19-l7"},
    {"id":"m19-c29","front":"Is pedal pulsing in ABS normal?","back":"Yes, during a hard stop on a slippery surface.","lesson":"m19-l7"},
    {"id":"m19-c30","front":"What does a lit ABS light mean?","back":"Normal brakes work, but without anti-lock.","lesson":"m19-l7"},
    {"id":"m19-c31","front":"What does ESC do?","back":"It brakes single wheels to correct a skid.","lesson":"m19-l7"},
    {"id":"m19-c32","front":"What is wheel slip?","back":"(Vehicle speed − wheel speed) ÷ vehicle speed. The best braking is at about 10 to 20%.","lesson":"m19-l8"},
    {"id":"m19-c33","front":"ABS valve states?","back":"Apply: inlet open, outlet closed. Hold: both closed. Release: inlet closed, outlet open.","lesson":"m19-l8"},
    {"id":"m19-c34","front":"ESC sensors?","back":"Steering angle, yaw rate, lateral acceleration, wheel speeds and brake pressure.","lesson":"m19-l8"},
    {"id":"m19-c35","front":"What causes a spongy pedal?","back":"Air in the lines or old, wet fluid.","lesson":"m19-l9"},
    {"id":"m19-c36","front":"What causes a hard pedal?","back":"A booster or vacuum fault.","lesson":"m19-l9"},
    {"id":"m19-c37","front":"What causes pulling when braking?","back":"A seized caliper, uneven pads or tyre faults.","lesson":"m19-l9"},
    {"id":"m19-c38","front":"What causes pedal pulsation?","back":"Disc thickness variation, runout or loose wheels.","lesson":"m19-l9"},
    {"id":"m19-c39","front":"What is brake fade?","back":"Loss of braking power from overheated brakes or fluid.","lesson":"m19-l9"},
    {"id":"m19-c40","front":"Braking efficiency formula?","back":"Total braking force ÷ vehicle weight × 100. A common minimum is 50%.","lesson":"m19-l10"},
    {"id":"m19-c41","front":"Roller test imbalance?","back":"The difference between left and right, as a percentage. A typical limit is about 25 to 30%.","lesson":"m19-l10"},
    {"id":"m19-c42","front":"Which bearing is noisy if it hums louder in a left turn?","back":"The right one, as load moves to the right in a left turn.","lesson":"m19-l10"},
    {"id":"m19-c43","front":"What does a growl that changes with speed show?","back":"A failing wheel bearing.","lesson":"m19-l11"},
    {"id":"m19-c44","front":"Can a sealed hub unit be adjusted?","back":"No. It is replaced as a whole unit.","lesson":"m19-l11"},
    {"id":"m19-c45","front":"Why is hub torque important?","back":"Too tight overheats the bearing and too loose lets it move.","lesson":"m19-l11"},
    {"id":"m19-c46","front":"What must be checked on a brake job at the tyres?","back":"Tread, pressure and sidewalls.","lesson":"m19-l11"}
  ]
});
